WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.546
Alex is planning a birthday at Juniper House. Add the enquiry at

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:09.092
the main desk. The conversation becomes five booking details, ready for staff

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:09.850
to check.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.708
Alex sends a voice message: six guests, sometime after eight. Review the

00:00:15.708 --> 00:00:20.916
transcript, then add it. The guest count changes; the exact time stays

00:00:20.916 --> 00:00:21.350
unresolved.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.966
Ask the desk assistant for a warm reply suggesting eight-thirty. Review the

00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:31.931
draft and place it in the staff composer. The assistant can also

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.000
propose follow-ups and shift notes.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.660
Alex accepts eight-thirty. Open Time to check the offer and acceptance. The

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.000
brief keeps its supporting messages and change history.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.700
Review the birthday, quiet table and peanut allergy against Alex’s words. Keep

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.750
the requests, approve the brief, and save the enquiry to the team.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.473
In Service planner, the dinner appears at eight-thirty with six guests and

00:00:59.473 --> 00:01:03.700
their requests. Follow-ups give the next shift a clear task.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.100
Assign an owner, priority and due time. Team comments keep the handoff

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.850
together, and the enquiry book makes the saved conversation easy to reopen.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.822
The website assistant answers from approved restaurant information. A private review page

00:01:19.822 --> 00:01:24.700
lets Alex check the request and return corrections to the team.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:32.040
Finally, explore email, WhatsApp and reservation handoffs in simulation. No real table

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.500
is reserved. Bring your own conversations to FormBridge.
